        fp: "An industrial polymer producer sent a signed, written purchase order on standard corporate letterhead to a chemical additive refiner: 'Please ship 5,000 drums of plasticizer additive Grade-X at $100 per drum ($500,000 total). Delivery on or before October 1. Terms: Net 30.' The purchase order was silent regarding dispute resolution and remedies. The refiner immediately transmitted a signed written acknowledgment stating: 'We accept your order for 5,000 drums of Grade-X at $100 per drum, delivery October 1, Net 30. Any controversy arising out of this contract shall be submitted to binding commercial arbitration in accordance with AAA rules. Notice of any nonconformity must be made within 48 hours of delivery or all claims are waived.' The producer received the acknowledgment, placed it in its file, and did not object. On October 1, the refiner delivered 5,000 drums. The producer began using the additive, but thirty days later discovered that the additive caused severe polymer embrittlement due to improper synthesis. The producer filed a civil breach of warranty action in state court. The refiner moved to stay the suit and compel arbitration, also asserting that the producer's failure to notify within 48 hours barred all claims.",
